---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import { loadSnippet, loadOutput, highlightJava } from '../../lib/snippets';

// The featured example is the REAL, tested ExtractToFolder snippet (verified by ExtractToFolderTest),
// extracted at build time exactly like the /snippets page. Its output is byte-identical to the other
// extract snippets (same simple.zip fixture), so we reuse the shared ExtractItems output fragment.
const extractToFolderCode = await highlightJava(loadSnippet('ExtractToFolder'));
const extractToFolderOut = loadOutput('ExtractItems');
const extractNestedCode = await highlightJava(loadSnippet('ExtractNestedArchive'));
const extractNestedOut = loadOutput('ExtractNestedArchive');
---
<Base
  title="Performance & correct usage — 7-Zip-JBinding"
  description="Why 7-Zip-JBinding extraction can turn slow, and how to keep it fast: extract in a single pass (O(n)) even on solid 7z archives, and write a callback that opens each output file exactly once."
>
  <div class="container perf">
    <div class="kicker">Documentation</div>
    <h1 class="perf-h1">Performance &amp; correct usage</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      7-Zip-JBinding is a thin JNI layer over the same native engine as the <code>7z</code> command
      line — extraction is essentially as fast as 7-Zip itself. When people report that extraction is
      &ldquo;slow&rdquo; (minutes or even an hour for an archive that <code>7z x</code> unpacks in
      seconds), the cause is almost always in the <b>calling code</b>, not the library. This page
      explains the two traps that cause it and shows a complete, tested example that avoids both.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
      <b>TL;DR</b> — Extract everything in <b>one</b> call to
      <code>inArchive.extract(indices, false, callback)</code>, and in your callback open each output
      file <b>once</b> in <code>getStream()</code>, keep appending in <code>write()</code>, and close
      it in <code>setOperationResult()</code>. Do no per-chunk work in the hot path.
    </div>

    <!-- ===================== 1: the O(n^2) trap ===================== -->
    <section class="perf-sec">
      <h2>1. The big one: don&rsquo;t extract solid archives item-by-item</h2>
      <p>
        7z archives are usually <b>solid</b>: many files are compressed together into one continuous
        stream, which is what gives 7z its great ratio. The price is that to reach file <i>n</i> the
        engine must decompress everything before it. That is fine when you extract in order in a
        single pass — the block is streamed exactly <b>once</b>.
      </p>
      <p>
        It becomes a disaster if you loop over items and extract them one at a time with
        <code>getStream(...).extractSlow(...)</code> (or a per-item <code>extract</code> call).
        Each call restarts decompression from the beginning of the solid block, so unpacking
        <i>k</i> files re-decompresses the block <i>k</i> times:
      </p>
      <div class="cmp">
        <div class="cmp-bad">
          <div class="cmp-tag">✗ O(n²) — re-decompresses every time</div>
          <pre><code>{`for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) {
    inArchive.extractSlow(i, out); // restarts the solid block each iteration
}`}</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="cmp-good">
          <div class="cmp-tag">✓ O(n) — one pass over the solid block</div>
          <pre><code>{`int[] all = ...; // indices of every file
inArchive.extract(all, false, callback); // engine streams the block once`}</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        On a solid archive with thousands of files this is the difference between seconds and an
        hour. Always hand the <b>full list of indices</b> to a single
        <code>extract(int[], false, callback)</code> call and let the engine walk the archive once.
        (<code>extractSlow</code> is a convenience for pulling out <b>one</b> item; it is the wrong
        tool for bulk extraction.)
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ===================== 2: the callback ===================== -->
    <section class="perf-sec">
      <h2>2. Write the callback the way the engine expects</h2>
      <p>
        The engine drives extraction through your <code>IArchiveExtractCallback</code>. It is a small
        state machine, and fighting it is the second common cause of slowness and bugs. Follow these
        rules:
      </p>
      <ul class="rules">
        <li>
          <b>Open each output file once, in <code>getStream()</code>.</b> This method is called once
          per item, right before its data starts. That is the place to create the target file and its
          parent directories — not inside the write loop.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b><code>write(byte[])</code> is called many times per file — just append.</b> Never
          re-open or seek to the end on every chunk. Re-opening the file for every buffer (a common
          mistake) turns linear extraction into an avalanche of syscalls. And you <b>must</b> return
          the number of bytes consumed (always the whole buffer).
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Close the file in <code>setOperationResult()</code>.</b> It is called once per item, after
          the last chunk — one open and one close per file, never per chunk.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Handle failure without hiding it.</b> When an item fails (say the disk fills mid-write),
          7-Zip-JBinding <b>saves that exception and re-throws it from <code>extract()</code></b>, so the
          real cause surfaces at the call site — print it with
          <code>SevenZipException.printStackTraceExtended()</code>. In the callback, be careful:
          <code>setOperationResult()</code> still runs for the failed item (with a non-OK result) and
          extraction <i>continues</i> to the next one, so on a failed item close the broken file
          <b>quietly</b> — a <code>close()</code> that throws &ldquo;cannot close&rdquo; would <b>mask</b>
          the real &ldquo;no space left on device&rdquo;. On a <i>successful</i> item, do let
          <code>close()</code> throw: that is where the final buffered block is flushed, and a failure
          there is genuine data loss. And close any still-open file in a <code>finally</code> at the
          call site too — belt and braces, in case an abort path skips the callback.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Keep the hot path empty.</b> Per-chunk logging, hashing, or flushing dominates the run
          time. Do progress reporting from <code>setCompleted()</code>, not from <code>write()</code>.
          (The example below computes a hash only to make its output verifiable in the test suite —
          real code should drop it.)
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Buffer your output.</b> Wrap the file stream in a <code>BufferedOutputStream</code> so
          the many small chunks don&rsquo;t each hit the disk.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- ===================== 3: single-thread note ===================== -->
    <section class="perf-sec">
      <h2>3. One archive, one thread</h2>
      <p>
        A single <code>IInArchive</code> instance and its extraction are <b>not</b> thread-safe, and
        the native codecs already use multiple cores internally where it helps. Don&rsquo;t try to
        speed a single archive up by extracting different items from several threads — it will not go
        faster and can corrupt state. To process many <i>separate</i> archives in parallel, give each
        thread its own <code>IInArchive</code>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ===================== featured snippet ===================== -->
    <section class="perf-sec">
      <h2>Complete example: extract a whole archive to a directory</h2>
      <p class="snip-desc">
        This puts all of the above together: it collects every non-folder index and extracts them in
        one pass, writing each file to disk under a target directory. It is a real program from the
        test suite — the output shown below is verified on every release.
      </p>
      <div class="codebox">
        <div class="codebox-head">
          <span>ExtractToFolder.java</span>
          <span class="head-right">
            <span class="badge"><span class="dot"></span> verified by ExtractToFolderTest</span>
            <button class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
          </span>
        </div>
        <div class="jd-code" set:html={extractToFolderCode} />
      </div>
      {extractToFolderOut && (
        <div class="codebox outbox">
          <div class="codebox-head"><span>Output</span></div>
          <Code code={extractToFolderOut} lang="text" themes={{ light: 'github-light', dark: 'one-dark-pro' }} />
        </div>
      )}
      <p class="more">
        More runnable examples are on the <a href="/snippets/">Code snippets</a> page.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ===================== wrapped archives ===================== -->
    <section class="perf-sec">
      <h2>Bonus: extract a wrapped archive (<code>.tar.gz</code>) in one go</h2>
      <p>
        A <code>.tar.gz</code> is not one archive but two: a GZip stream whose single item is a
        <code>.tar</code>, which in turn holds the real files. So unwrapping it is always two
        open/extract steps — open the outer GZip, pull out the inner <code>.tar</code>, then open
        that. The decision is <b>where the intermediate archive goes</b>:
      </p>
      <ul class="rules">
        <li>
          <b>In memory</b> — keep the inner archive in a <code>ByteArrayStream</code>. No temp file,
          simplest, great as long as the inner archive comfortably fits in memory.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Via a temporary file</b> — stream the inner archive to disk and reopen it. Bounded
          memory; the safe default for a large inner archive.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Streamed, never materialised (advanced)</b> — you do <b>not</b> have to hold the whole
          inner archive anywhere first. Decompress the GZip on one thread and let the <code>.tar</code>
          reader on another thread <b>pull</b> those bytes on demand through a small in-memory pipe:
          extraction of the inner files starts immediately, overlapping with decompression, and the two
          run concurrently. It is genuinely tricky — the tar reader asks for the total size up front
          (answer it cheaply from the GZip <code>ISIZE</code> trailer) and seeks backward to re-read
          data blocks (so the pipe needs a look-behind cache). Because it is advanced we don't inline
          it here, but it is real and proven: see the test
          <code>ExtractNestedArchiveStreamingTest</code> for a complete, back-pressured implementation.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="snip-desc">
        The example below shows the first two. The same pattern works for any nesting
        (<code>.cpio.gz</code>, a <code>.zip</code> inside a <code>.7z</code>, …).
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        <b>Streams, done right.</b> This is a master-class example, not a copy-paste starter, so it
        treats streams with the care production code needs: every <code>Closeable</code> is managed
        with <b>try-with-resources</b>, and a failing <code>close()</code> on the <b>write</b> path
        (say the last buffered block can't be flushed because the disk is full) is allowed to
        propagate — never swallowed. A swallowed <code>close()</code> is silent data corruption.
      </div>
      <div class="codebox">
        <div class="codebox-head">
          <span>ExtractNestedArchive.java</span>
          <span class="head-right">
            <span class="badge"><span class="dot"></span> verified by ExtractNestedArchiveTest</span>
            <button class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
          </span>
        </div>
        <div class="jd-code" set:html={extractNestedCode} />
      </div>
      {extractNestedOut && (
        <div class="codebox outbox">
          <div class="codebox-head"><span>Output</span></div>
          <Code code={extractNestedOut} lang="text" themes={{ light: 'github-light', dark: 'one-dark-pro' }} />
        </div>
      )}
    </section>
  </div>

  <style>
    .perf { max-width: 860px; padding-top: 40px; padding-bottom: 40px; }
    .perf-h1 { font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 4vw, 2.6rem); margin-bottom: 8px; }
    .perf .lead { font-size: 1.05rem; }
    .perf-sec { margin: 40px 0; }
    .perf-sec h2 {
      margin: 0 0 12px; padding-top: 26px; border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
      font-size: 1.3rem;
    }
    .perf-sec p { margin: 0 0 14px; }
    .perf code {
      font: 600 .86em var(--font-mono); background: var(--surface-2);
      border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 5px; padding: 1px 5px;
    }
    .callout {
      background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--border-strong);
      border-left: 3px solid var(--blue); border-radius: 8px;
      padding: 14px 18px; margin: 24px 0; font-size: .96rem; line-height: 1.6;
    }
    .callout code { background: var(--surface-2); }

    .cmp { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 14px; margin: 18px 0; }
    .cmp-bad, .cmp-good {
      border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 8px; overflow: hidden; background: var(--surface);
    }
    .cmp-tag { font: 600 .78rem var(--font-sans); padding: 8px 12px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); }
    .cmp-bad .cmp-tag { color: #e06c75; }
    .cmp-good .cmp-tag { color: #98c379; }
    .cmp pre { margin: 0; padding: 12px 14px; overflow: auto; font-size: .8rem; }
    .cmp code { background: transparent; border: 0; padding: 0; font-weight: 500; color: var(--text-2); }
    @media (max-width: 720px) { .cmp { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }

    .rules { margin: 0 0 14px; padding-left: 20px; }
    .rules li { margin-bottom: 12px; line-height: 1.6; }

    .snip-desc { font-size: .96rem; }
    .codebox { margin: 14px 0 10px; }
    .codebox pre, .jd-code pre { max-height: 620px; overflow: auto; margin: 0; padding: 16px 18px; background: transparent !important; font-size: .86rem; }
    .codebox-head {
      display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between;
      padding: 8px 14px; font: 600 .82rem var(--font-mono); color: var(--text-3);
      background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-bottom: 0;
      border-radius: 8px 8px 0 0;
    }
    .jd-code, .codebox :global(pre.astro-code) {
      border: 1px solid var(--border); border-top: 0; border-radius: 0 0 8px 8px;
    }
    .jd-code a.jd-link { color: inherit; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 1px dotted var(--border-strong); }
    .jd-code a.jd-link:hover { border-bottom-color: var(--blue); color: var(--blue) !important; }
    .jd-code a.jd-link:hover * { color: var(--blue) !important; }
    .outbox .codebox-head { color: var(--amber-soft); }
    .head-right { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px; }
    .badge {
      display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 6px;
      font-size: .7rem; padding: 2px 9px; border-radius: 999px;
      border: 1px solid var(--border-strong); color: var(--text-2);
    }
    .badge .dot { width: 7px; height: 7px; border-radius: 50%; background: #3fb950; }
    .copy-btn {
      background: transparent; border: 1px solid var(--border-strong); color: var(--text-2);
      border-radius: 6px; padding: 2px 10px; font: 600 .74rem var(--font-sans); cursor: pointer;
    }
    .copy-btn:hover { color: var(--text); border-color: var(--blue); }
    .more { margin-top: 16px; font-size: .95rem; }
    .more a { color: var(--blue); }
  </style>

  <script>
    document.querySelectorAll('[data-copy]').forEach((btn) => {
      btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        const pre = btn.closest('.codebox')?.querySelector('pre');
        if (!pre) return;
        navigator.clipboard.writeText(pre.textContent ?? '');
        btn.textContent = 'Copied!';
        setTimeout(() => (btn.textContent = 'Copy'), 1600);
      });
    });
  </script>
</Base>
